Hi,

In my original example writing

    \DeclareDocumentCommand \foo { ?{\my_sanitise:n} } {
      % do whatever \foo is supposed to do with #1
    }

I neglected to include how \my_sanitise:n would actually be written. =20
And I'm not too sure about that. If it ends up that \my_sanitise:n has =20=

to write to a scratch variable anyway, then I'm not sure we've saved =20
too much.

I think I'd like to see some more refinements of the idea before =20
discussing the syntax further.

On 11/08/2009, at 2:23 AM, Manuel P=E9gouri=E9-Gonnard wrote:

> I think that if you want to support this in xparse then it should be =20=

> accessible
> for all argument types. In my opinion, argument parsing and argument
> preprocessing are orthogonal things, and the syntax should reflect =20
> this
> orthogonality.

[...]

> If I'm not mistaken, currently the plan is to allow a '+' modifier =20
> in front of
> every specifier in order to allow for \par in the argument. Maybe it =20=

> is possible
> to add another modifier for preprocessing.

Good thinking. I prefer another modifier over an optional argument. =20
Does '>\preprocess' work for you? (I like having only a single token =20
there, but you'd be able to convince me otherwise.)

> Of course it is always hard to find the right balance between =20
> simplicity and
> features, but in this case my personal preference would be to have =20
> it included.

I think adding this feature, possibly as "experimental" for now, would =20=

be a good idea.

* * *

In terms of offering expandable optional arguments, I think Joseph's =20
"wright" that this can't possibly work with more complex argument =20
types; I might be wrong, however. Perhaps this can be something to =20
revisit with LuaTeX.

Will


